    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters July 6 with a lineup anchored by two high-upside plays. **James Wood** (78.28) is our strongest start today against Burrows, bolstered by an elite 0.425 xwOBA and a favorable matchup against a starter with a 4.71 xERA. His leadoff position maximizes his counting stat potential. Close behind is **Corbin Carroll** (73.02), who faces Walker Buehler. While the park factors against **Corbin Carroll** are slightly punitive, his dynamic platoon advantage and current high-tier projection make him an essential engine for our stolen base production, a critical need given our aggressive roster strategy. **Isaac Paredes** (72.13) also warrants spotlighting; his matchup against Mikolas offers a massive +14% boost due to SP skill, allowing him to anchor the corner infield with strong power-hitting potential.\n\nOur lineup construction hinges on exploiting specific SP vulnerabilities, most notably through **Mickey Moniak** (61.81) and **Kyle Karros** (60.80), both of whom benefit significantly from facing the struggling Eric Lauer. **Mickey Moniak** receives a massive +19% bump from Lauer\u2019s high xERA, which effectively masks the inherent volatility in his dynamic platoon split. While **Austin Riley** (60.04) faces a tougher test in Freddy Peralta, his score remains elevated by Peralta\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a sneaky opportunity for us to push for stolen bases despite the \"marginal\" risk of rain. We are monitoring the weather in that contest closely; a postponement would force us to pivot to our deeper bench reserves.\n\nRegarding roster management, the most difficult decision was keeping **Jose Caballero** (42.73) in the final utility slot. His sub-50 score reflects a punishing dynamic platoon disadvantage (-13%), and he sat right on the bubble of the benching threshold. He remains active only because we are prioritizing his potential for late-game speed should the matchup against Jax shift in our favor. Looking at the inactive list, we continue to manage the absence of **Josh Naylor**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, all of whom remain sidelined or unavailable. We are keeping a close watch on the recovery timelines for **Seiya Suzuki**, **Trevor Story**, and **Max Muncy** to see if they can provide the necessary depth to eventually displace lower-scoring starters like **Dalton Rushing** (42.54), whose efficiency today is hampered by the L/L matchup against Kyle Freeland. Prospects like **Henry Bolte**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Cole Young**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana** remain on our radar for future call-ups if these starters fail to meet our daily efficiency floor.",
